WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.833
Chat is where you ask;
projects are where the work lives.

2
00:00:04.833 --> 00:00:07.510
This episode: shared files, honest boards,

3
00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:12.417
discussions next to the tasks — and an
agent picking up work while you watch.

4
00:00:13.617 --> 00:00:16.247
This is the website relaunch, mid-flight.

5
00:00:16.247 --> 00:00:20.801
Columns tell the truth: what waits,
what runs, what is stuck in review.

6
00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:25.484
People and agents share this board —
the avatars tell you who holds what.

7
00:00:26.684 --> 00:00:29.644
Every project carries
its own shelf of context:

8
00:00:29.644 --> 00:00:32.541
the content inventory,
the launch-day runbook.

9
00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:37.013
Agents working inside this project
read these first — scoped knowledge,

10
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:39.155
exactly like episode three taught.

11
00:00:40.355 --> 00:00:43.755
Decisions live next to the
work, not in a lost thread.

12
00:00:43.755 --> 00:00:48.981
Open questions get a discussion; mention
an agent with an at-sign and it joins with

13
00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:51.185
the project context already loaded.

14
00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:54.848
Now the centerpiece.

15
00:00:54.848 --> 00:00:58.880
We create a task the usual
way — a title, to do, save.

16
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.747
Nothing special about it.

17
00:01:00.747 --> 00:01:06.795
Except this workspace runs the triage
automation from last episode… so watch what

18
00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:07.765
happens next.

19
00:01:08.965 --> 00:01:11.503
The automation scored it, judged it a fit,

20
00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:14.705
and assigned it — the
agent’s name lands on the card.

21
00:01:14.705 --> 00:01:18.149
Open it, and the reasoning
sits right there as a comment:

22
00:01:18.149 --> 00:01:20.203
why this agent, in plain language.

23
00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:25.097
Had the score been low, there would be
only a suggestion — and the decision would

24
00:01:25.097 --> 00:01:26.245
stay with a person.

25
00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:30.830
The backlog closes the loop.

26
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:36.424
Agents can propose work they think is
needed — but proposals sit here until a person

27
00:01:36.424 --> 00:01:37.356
promotes them.

28
00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:38.888
Initiative stays human.

29
00:01:38.888 --> 00:01:42.285
The AI suggests; you
decide what enters the sprint.

30
00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:47.821
And each project chooses its own
crew: which agents may work here,

31
00:01:47.821 --> 00:01:49.529
which models they may use.

32
00:01:49.529 --> 00:01:54.325
A project is a trust boundary too —
staff it deliberately, like any team.

33
00:01:55.525 --> 00:01:58.429
Projects: shared context, an honest board,

34
00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:03.408
agents that take work and propose it
— with people deciding what counts.

35
00:02:03.408 --> 00:02:08.525
Next episode: integrations — where
your workspace meets the outside world.

36
00:02:09.725 --> 00:02:13.381
The projects section of the
documentation covers boards,

37
00:02:13.381 --> 00:02:15.862
backlogs, and task automation in full.

38
00:02:15.862 --> 00:02:17.495
See you in episode seven.

